# I spray painted an xbox controller!
My partner wanted bunny themed gifts for their birthday, and they wanted an xbox controller. I decided to combine the two to surprise them with a bunny themed xbox controller. I looked into custom printed xbox controller faceplate's, but such a thing doesn't really exist. I found one person online who would hydro dip a custom printed sheet for you on an xbox faceplate, but he had a really long turnaround time so that was off the table. I landed on the idea that I would just spray paint my own (despite having no experience).

## My setup
I 3d printed a holder arm to hold up the controller via a screw in the back of the faceplate. My mother had the idea to cut the face out of a box to catch the over spray, and to elevate the controller a little bit with an old mug. It was around 40 degrees out, so we also used a space heater to help with the drying process.
![Spray paint setup](/resources/blog/spray_setup.jpg)

## How I did it
I started with a couple layers of primer in order to help ensure the next layers of paint stick well. The advice I heard is to not rely on paint + primer 2 in one paint. Apparently it just won't hold as well as a separate primer will.

Then, it was time for my base color. I choose a light pink. Word of warning to anyone who uses a light colored spray paint, it won't look like the surface is being painted at all at first. It will take a few layers for you to see an even coat. I moved too close on my first coat because I didn't see the surface changing color at all, which caused a lot of dripping. I quickly padded off the dripping with a paper towel & it took a lot of extra layers to make it look even again.

Next, I attached my stencils. I made stencils using my Silhouette Portrait 3, a small desktop cutting machine. At first, I tried cutting them on a weak vinyl. It was too strong though and ended up pulling off parts of my nice pink coat (and I had to redo my pink layers again!).
![Controller next to stencil cutout](/resources/blog/spray_pre-stencil.jpg)
*Sheet of cut vinyl stencils, pre-disaster*

I ended up buying a thin clear sheet to use as a stencil instead, which I attached using a light tack spray adhesive.
![Stencils next to adhesive spray can](/resources/blog/spray_prepped_stencils.jpg)
*The stencils, sprayed and ready to be attached to the controller*

To make sure the paint wouldn't bleed beyond the edges of the stencils, I tested it on a separate controller first. I wanted to avoid more disasters as much as possible. I was also worried about putting any adhesives on the painted controller, so I attempted to cover the whole surface without directly taping to the face. I used paper towels in this test, and only taped them to the back of the controller, to the stencils, and to each other.
![Painted test controller with masking](/resources/blog/spray_painted_test.jpg)
*My test controller, with the masking applied and painted*

It worked!
![Painted test controller without masking](/resources/blog/spray_final_test.jpg)

I learned a couple of tricks, first on the curved surfaces the stencils would kink. I made a cut on the back of the large bunny stencils, that way the stencil would overlap it's self instead of kinking up. Second, from other testing I learned to do a few very thin coats. Any running of the paint, even minor amounts small to see will cause it to run under the stencil and cause a smear. Third, the adhesive seems to loosen over time. It's best if I get the job done relatively quick after applying the stencils

On to the real thing! I decided to go with tin foil instead of paper towels, at it keeps its shape easier. I had my workstation prepped so I could mask out the controller as quick as possible after applying the stencils.
![My station prepared, with precut masking tape and sheets of tinfoil](/resources/blog/spray_prepped_station.jpg)

And on to the painting!